# xorbits.mpc
#
# Pi_xorBits^(q) (paper "Better Modulus Conversion for MPC and Threshold
# FHE", Damgaard/Hasler/Kolby/Pawlak, Section 5 / Protocol xorBits, the
# "else" branch for a non-2-power prime q -- which is our case, q and t are
# both odd primes).
#
# On input x_1, ..., x_N in {0,1}^n from the N parties (one length-n bit
# vector per party), this computes n independent sharings of
# XOR_sigma x_sigma, entirely inside ONE arithmetic domain q. It is the
# per-domain combination step that a MultiBit is built from: run this once
# per modulus (q, t, and later an auxiliary large Q for the consistency
# check) on correlated inputs and you get a bit shared simultaneously in
# each domain.
#
# Two things the paper leaves to a sub-protocol are simplified here and
# documented as such (see xorbits_README.md):
#   - CheckedInput (a batched, FLIOP-based proof that n bits are all really
#     0/1) is replaced by the direct, unbatched check open(x*(1-x)) == 0 for
#     every bit of every party, aborting otherwise. This is real MPC (the
#     check reveals nothing beyond "this was a bit", since x*(1-x) is always
#     literally 0 for a genuine bit) but costs one multiplication + opening
#     per bit instead of O(1) amortized per batch.
#   - The XOR combination u xor v := u + v - 2*Mul(u,v) uses MP-SPDZ's
#     native sint multiplication directly, which already *is* an
#     implementation of a secure multiplication protocol (Beaver triples
#     under the hood) -- there's no need to hand-roll Beaver here.
#
# Compile-time args: <n_parties> <n_bits> [<reveal_for_test>]
#   reveal_for_test (default 0): if 1, also reveal+print the resulting bits,
#   for standalone correctness testing against xorbits_gen_inputs.py's
#   expected output. A real caller (MultiBitsConsCheck) never does this --
#   the whole point is that the bits stay secret.
#
# Example:
#   ./compile.py -M -P 8095387678513 xorbits 3 20 1
#   python3 Scripts/xorbits_gen_inputs.py 3 20 --seed 1
#   Scripts/setup-ssl.sh 3
#   for i in 0 1 2; do
#     ./shamir-party.x $i xorbits-3-20-1 -N 3 -P 8095387678513 -OF . &
#   done

program.use_split(False)

n_parties = int(program.args[1])
n_bits = int(program.args[2])
reveal_for_test = int(program.args[3]) if len(program.args) > 3 else 0

assert n_parties >= 2
assert n_bits >= 1

# --- Step 1: each party inputs its bit vector, one sint per (party, bit). ---
x = [[sint.get_input_from(sigma) for k in range(n_bits)]
     for sigma in range(n_parties)]

# --- Bitness check (stand-in for CheckedInput): open x*(1-x) for every
# single bit of every party, abort if any is nonzero. This leaks nothing
# about the x's beyond "all inputs were really bits", since a genuine bit
# always makes x*(1-x) = 0 regardless of its value.
#
# Note these must be checked *individually*, not summed into one opened
# value first: a malicious party could otherwise pick several non-bit
# inputs whose individual checks are nonzero but happen to cancel out mod
# q, so that their sum opens to 0 while the bitness property still fails. ---
checks = Array(n_parties * n_bits, sint)
idx = 0
for sigma in range(n_parties):
    for k in range(n_bits):
        checks[idx] = x[sigma][k] * (1 - x[sigma][k])
        idx += 1
opened_checks = checks.reveal_list()
for v in opened_checks:
    runtime_error_if(v != 0,
                      'bitness check failed: some input was not in {0,1}')

# --- Step 2: combine via sequential secure XOR, b = x_1 (xor) x_2 (xor) ...
# Each xor of two shared bits costs exactly one secure multiplication
# (Beaver triple, 1 round to open the two masked values) -- see the
# discussion of why this can't be done locally. N-1 multiplications total,
# done here as N-1 *sequential* rounds (each xor depends on the previous
# one's result); MP-SPDZ still batches the n_bits of one round into a
# single vectorized multiplication instruction. ---
b = x[0]
for sigma in range(1, n_parties):
    prod = [b[k] * x[sigma][k] for k in range(n_bits)]
    b = [b[k] + x[sigma][k] - 2 * prod[k] for k in range(n_bits)]

if reveal_for_test:
    b_arr = Array(n_bits, sint)
    for k in range(n_bits):
        b_arr[k] = b[k]
    opened_b = b_arr.reveal_list()
    for k in range(n_bits):
        print_ln('xorbits[%s] = %s', k, opened_b[k])
